# bershka-scraper — configuration
# ===============================
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored; this file is not, so it
# must never hold a real value. env_config.py reads exactly the four names
# below and warns about anything else, which catches a typo like
# TWO_CAPTCHA_KEY before it becomes a confusing empty run.
#
# Credentials live HERE and nowhere else — never on a command line. `ps`
# reads argv, and argv lands in shell history and in CI logs.

# 2captcha.com API key. Needed by scraper_api_client.py, by
# fingerprint_client.py and by tools/browser_profile_client.py. NOT needed by
# api_scraper.py.
TWOCAPTCHA_KEY=

# An HTTP proxy, as a single URL: http://user:pass@host:port
#
# THIS IS NOT OPTIONAL FOR DEVELOPMENT. Measured 2026-09-19 from a Moscow
# residential address, every URL on www.bershka.com answered HTTP 403 in 195
# bytes — /robots.txt included. Through a proxy the same URLs answered 200.
# api_scraper.py warns when this is unset rather than letting a run discover
# it halfway through.
#
# One credential is enough for several exits: `--proxy-sessions N` turns it
# into that many session-pinned addresses. A pool of distinct exits goes in a
# `proxylist.txt` passed to `--proxy-file`; .gitignore already covers
# `proxylist*.txt`, and .github/ci_checks.py will still refuse to let one be
# committed, which is the behaviour you want.
#
# Note on session pinning, measured 2026-09-20: a session-pinned exit holds
# one address for the session lifetime and then moves. A run that was getting
# 200s began getting the 403 above mid-session. `--proxy-file` with a real
# pool is the more robust shape for a long run.
BERSHKA_PROXY=

# A Scraping Browser CDP endpoint, for the three browser engines only.
# api_scraper.py and scraper_api_client.py do not use it.
#
# Mint one for THIS repo rather than reusing a sibling's:
#   python3 tools/browser_profile_client.py accounts
#   python3 tools/browser_profile_client.py use --account-id N --write-env
#
# To attach your own exit to the vendor's browser instead of theirs:
#   python3 tools/browser_profile_client.py connection --account-id N \
#       --proxy-line 1 --write-env
#
# Measured 2026-09-20: the vendor's own exit answered `500 proxy_timeout`,
# and with a custom proxy attached it answered `500 browser_timeout`, twice —
# then connected in 3.0s on the next attempt with no change. Transient
# vendor-side failure, not a configuration error.
#
# A profile allows ONE live connection; a second run on the same profile
# fails with `profile_locked`.
BERSHKA_CDP_ENDPOINT=

# Optional. Overrides the URL an engine would otherwise build.
BERSHKA_URL=
